      <workList xml:id="workDesc_7e52ab4b">
         <work xml:id="work_id061e9872">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_94f458f9">C4:008</identifier>
            <title xml:id="title_id6a4f92d4" xml:lang="de">Lustspiele der Jungfer Charlotta Dorothea Biehl</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_cea08348">
               <persName role="author" xml:id="persName_id6019187b">Charlotta Dorothea Biehl</persName>
               <persName role="translator" xml:id="persName_8bf0fa6b">Johann Adolph Scheibe</persName>
            </contributor>
            <creation xml:id="creation_id9cc4216c">
               <date isodate="1769" xml:id="date_efa432ce">1769</date>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_ide71a06d7">
               <p xml:id="idm64">Though Scheibe was a good friend of the playwright <persName xml:id="idm65" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Ludvig Holberg</persName>, he had not translated any of his comedies. Presumably instigated by <persName xml:id="idm66" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Charlotta Dorothea Biehl</persName>, a playwright and translator herself, Scheibe made a German translation of some of her works. In his<rend xml:id="idm67" fontstyle="italic"> Anekdoten</rend> (SchW C3:001; 1771) Scheibe mentions that the Danes indeed have some poets and historians who do them honour; however, there are also some learned women ('lærde Fruentimmer') who ought to be mentioned: ‘Andersen, Biel, Beck’. The first volume in two parts including six plays appeared in 1767 (SchW C4:007). The present German edition of ‘Den Ædelmodige. Comedie i 5 Acter’ was published two years later in a volume together with a comedy by the playwright <persName xml:id="idm68" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Peder Topp Wandall</persName> (SchW C4:012). The edition does not indicate that Scheibe was the translator, but since he was the translator of the two volumes appearing in 1767 he was most likely also responsible for the present one. A very brief review appeared in <rend xml:id="idm69" fontstyle="italic">Allgemeine deutsche Bibliothek</rend> (appendix to 1771–91) mentioning that the work was not necessary to include in a German library though ‘the translation has not turned out bad’ (‘Die Uebersetzung ist nicht übel gerathen’).</p>
            </history>
